1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,680
Three messages. CI here means the automated
checks that GitHub Actions runs on each

2
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:13,327
change, and a commit is one
saved snapshot of the project.

3
00:00:13,667 --> 00:00:18,696
"The loader test passes on my Mac. In CI
it says the configuration file does not

4
00:00:18,736 --> 00:00:21,864
exist. The file is right
there in the repository."

5
00:00:22,204 --> 00:00:27,256
"The deploy script works when I run it. In
CI the very first line fails, something

6
00:00:27,296 --> 00:00:29,492
about bash and a strange character."

7
00:00:29,832 --> 00:00:33,845
"The version step works on
every laptop in the team.

8
00:00:33,885 --> 00:00:35,793
In CI it exits with 128."

9
00:00:36,133 --> 00:00:40,002
Each of them ends the message the same
way: "and it is the same commit."

10
00:00:40,042 --> 00:00:43,694
They're right about that. Same
commit, and still not the same input.

11
00:00:43,734 --> 00:00:48,091
The machine is different, the clone is
different, and in the first two cases what

12
00:00:48,131 --> 00:00:51,131
they have on their own disk
isn't what Git has recorded.

13
00:00:51,471 --> 00:00:55,706
All three can be proven on your own
machine, with Git alone, without re-running

14
00:00:55,746 --> 00:00:59,278
anything on GitHub. And keep the
phrase "the same commit" in mind.

15
00:00:59,318 --> 00:01:02,850
By the end you'll have four questions
to ask whenever you hear it.

16
00:01:04,690 --> 00:01:08,229
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

17
00:01:08,269 --> 00:01:11,398
In the last video you learned
the investigation order.

18
00:01:11,438 --> 00:01:16,269
This video is the catalogue that the order
leads you into: the documented causes of

19
00:01:16,309 --> 00:01:19,496
a test that passes locally
and fails on GitHub Actions.

20
00:01:19,836 --> 00:01:25,076
The textbook lists fourteen. Twelve are
GitHub Actions behavior, and you've met most

21
00:01:25,116 --> 00:01:30,104
of them in the last ten videos. Two are
Git. The demonstration takes the two Git

22
00:01:30,144 --> 00:01:35,257
causes, case sensitivity and line endings,
and adds the shallow clone, because all

23
00:01:35,297 --> 00:01:37,269
three can be reproduced locally.

24
00:01:37,609 --> 00:01:41,976
For each of the three the form is the
same: the symptom as the developer would

25
00:01:42,016 --> 00:01:46,270
report it, then the one command that
proves the cause, then the fix that Git

26
00:01:46,310 --> 00:01:46,722
records.

27
00:01:47,062 --> 00:01:51,786
One caveat about the transcripts. The
case-sensitivity demonstration depends on a

28
00:01:51,826 --> 00:01:54,844
case-insensitive volume,
which is the macOS default.

29
00:01:54,884 --> 00:01:59,313
On a case-sensitive volume the first
command fails and there is no collision

30
00:01:59,353 --> 00:01:59,783
warning.

31
00:02:01,623 --> 00:02:03,202
Here's what you'll walk away with.

32
00:02:03,542 --> 00:02:04,954
After this video you can:

33
00:02:05,294 --> 00:02:09,770
list the documented causes of a test that
passes on a Mac and fails on the runner;

34
00:02:10,110 --> 00:02:12,480
prove a case-sensitivity
cause with Git alone;

35
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:17,859
prove a line-ending cause with git
ls-files --eol and fix it with attributes;

36
00:02:18,199 --> 00:02:20,915
prove a shallow-clone cause
and give the minimal fix;

37
00:02:21,255 --> 00:02:24,108
explain why "same commit"
is not the same input.

38
00:02:25,948 --> 00:02:29,479
Here are the fourteen rows of the
textbook's table, in two pages.

39
00:02:29,819 --> 00:02:34,128
Go through the fourteen rows grouped by
where they sit in the investigation order.

40
00:02:34,468 --> 00:02:39,564
About the commit and the clone. A shallow,
tagless clone: the checkout fetches one

41
00:02:39,604 --> 00:02:43,697
commit and no tags, so git describe
and tag-derived versions fail.

42
00:02:43,737 --> 00:02:48,832
A tag is a fixed name for one commit, and
git describe builds a name from a tag in

43
00:02:48,872 --> 00:02:49,584
the history.

44
00:02:49,924 --> 00:02:54,526
The remedy is fetch-depth: 0. And the
job isn't testing the pushed commit: on

45
00:02:54,566 --> 00:02:59,410
pull_request it checks out the test merge
in detached HEAD, and it doesn't run at

46
00:02:59,450 --> 00:03:04,173
all while the pull request conflicts. The
remedy is to merge or rebase the base

47
00:03:04,213 --> 00:03:05,439
locally to reproduce.

48
00:03:05,779 --> 00:03:11,101
About authority. Missing secrets. A secret
is an encrypted value stored in GitHub's

49
00:03:11,141 --> 00:03:15,622
settings. Secrets aren't passed to
runs from forks or from Dependabot.

50
00:03:15,662 --> 00:03:19,692
The token is read-only. And an
unset secret is an empty string.

51
00:03:19,732 --> 00:03:25,053
The remedy is fork-safe jobs, and the
textbook adds: never pull_request_target as a

52
00:03:25,093 --> 00:03:25,634
shortcut.

53
00:03:25,974 --> 00:03:31,722
About the machine. Shell differences: the
implicit shell on Linux and macOS is bash

54
00:03:31,762 --> 00:03:37,510
-e without pipefail, and shell: bash adds
it. In a job container the default is sh.

55
00:03:37,550 --> 00:03:43,018
On Windows it's PowerShell. Moving images
and tools: weekly image rebuilds, and

56
00:03:43,058 --> 00:03:48,876
every -latest label moved in 2026. Out of
memory or time: smaller runners in private

57
00:03:48,916 --> 00:03:54,524
repositories, and the six-hour limit.
Environment differences: the variable CI is

58
00:03:54,564 --> 00:03:59,544
set to true, steps share no shell
state, and every job is a new machine.

59
00:03:59,884 --> 00:04:05,079
About what the run depends on. Old action
majors: actions written for Node 20 now

60
00:04:05,119 --> 00:04:10,508
run on Node 24. A stale or missing cache:
a cache is immutable per key, restore keys

61
00:04:10,548 --> 00:04:15,807
take the most recent prefix match, and pull
request caches are scoped to the merge

62
00:04:15,847 --> 00:04:16,066
ref.

63
00:04:16,406 --> 00:04:21,943
About whether a run exists. A required check
that stays pending, because a workflow

64
00:04:21,983 --> 00:04:27,521
skipped by a filter or by [skip ci] never
reports. Runs cancelled by a newer run in

65
00:04:27,561 --> 00:04:32,964
the same concurrency group. And a workflow
that never fires: events made with the

66
00:04:33,004 --> 00:04:38,541
job token start no runs, and scheduled
workflows are disabled after 60 days without

67
00:04:38,581 --> 00:04:41,431
repository activity in
public repositories.

68
00:04:41,771 --> 00:04:47,222
And the two that are Git. Case sensitivity:
macOS and Windows filesystems ignore

69
00:04:47,262 --> 00:04:52,920
case by default, and a Linux runner doesn't.
The remedy is to fix the names in Git.

70
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:57,862
Line endings: CRLF committed, or
converted on checkout by core.autocrlf.

71
00:04:57,902 --> 00:05:03,629
CRLF is a line ending of two characters, a
carriage return and then a line feed, and

72
00:05:03,669 --> 00:05:07,473
LF is the line feed alone.
The remedy is .gitattributes.

73
00:05:07,813 --> 00:05:12,709
The textbook attaches a caveat here and I
keep it. The official Actions pages read

74
00:05:12,749 --> 00:05:17,404
for the Phase 0 report don't state the
time zone and locale of hosted runners,

75
00:05:17,444 --> 00:05:22,040
whether Windows runners check out with
CRLF by default, or whether the hosted

76
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:26,976
runners' filesystems are case-sensitive.
Only the Git mechanisms are sourced, from

77
00:05:27,016 --> 00:05:31,852
Git's documentation and from real runs.
Linux filesystems being case-sensitive is

78
00:05:31,892 --> 00:05:34,200
the general rule the chapter relies on.

79
00:05:34,540 --> 00:05:39,486
Why the two Git causes happen at all. For
case: when Git creates a repository it

80
00:05:39,526 --> 00:05:44,722
tests the filesystem, and on a case-insensitive
one it sets core.ignorecase to true.

81
00:05:44,762 --> 00:05:49,896
Git's own records compare names byte by
byte. Those records are the index, which is

82
00:05:49,936 --> 00:05:54,821
the proposed next commit, and the trees,
the directory listings inside commits.

83
00:05:55,161 --> 00:05:59,999
So Git can hold a name with a capital
letter, your code can ask for the lower-case

84
00:06:00,039 --> 00:06:04,579
name, and on your Mac the file opens,
because the filesystem answers for both

85
00:06:04,619 --> 00:06:08,327
spellings. On a filesystem that
distinguishes case, it doesn't.

86
00:06:08,667 --> 00:06:12,679
For line endings: a blob, the stored
content of one file, is bytes.

87
00:06:12,719 --> 00:06:17,759
If the bytes contain carriage returns, every
checkout that doesn't convert them gets

88
00:06:17,799 --> 00:06:22,840
carriage returns. A shell script whose first
line ends in a carriage return asks the

89
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:26,468
kernel for an interpreter whose
name ends in that character.

90
00:06:26,808 --> 00:06:31,728
In both cases, the laptop is the forgiving
machine, and the forgiveness hides what

91
00:06:31,768 --> 00:06:32,878
is recorded in Git.

92
00:06:34,718 --> 00:06:36,231
Let's take that phrase apart.

93
00:06:36,571 --> 00:06:41,694
"Same commit" means: the same tree, the
same bytes in every blob, the same names.

94
00:06:41,734 --> 00:06:44,243
It says nothing about four other inputs.

95
00:06:44,583 --> 00:06:49,332
The filesystem that the names are written
onto. A name is recorded once in Git and

96
00:06:49,372 --> 00:06:51,727
interpreted by each machine's filesystem.

97
00:06:52,067 --> 00:06:55,696
How much of the repository came
along. The commit is the same.

98
00:06:55,736 --> 00:06:58,181
The history and the tags around it aren't.

99
00:06:58,521 --> 00:07:02,640
Which commit the job took. On a pull
request it's not your commit at all.

100
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:06,351
And the process that runs your
command: the shell and its options.

101
00:07:06,691 --> 00:07:11,002
So when someone says "it is the same
commit", agree, and then ask the four

102
00:07:11,042 --> 00:07:14,059
questions. There they are,
as promised at the start.

103
00:07:14,099 --> 00:07:18,703
For each there is a Git-side test that
needs no re-run of the workflow, and the

104
00:07:18,743 --> 00:07:20,702
demonstration shows three of them.

105
00:07:21,042 --> 00:07:25,649
Where this model has an edge: the first two
demonstrations aren't really differences

106
00:07:25,689 --> 00:07:30,296
between the Mac and the runner. They're
differences between what is on your disk and

107
00:07:30,336 --> 00:07:33,173
what is in Git. The runner
only sees what is in Git.

108
00:07:33,213 --> 00:07:37,820
That's why the proof in each case is a Git
command that reads the index or a commit,

109
00:07:37,860 --> 00:07:39,811
and not a command that reads a file.

110
00:07:41,651 --> 00:07:46,123
Try it now. Thirty seconds, in any
repository on your own machine.

111
00:07:46,163 --> 00:07:49,747
Run git config get
core.ignorecase, which only reads.

112
00:07:49,787 --> 00:07:51,046
What does it print?

113
00:07:53,386 --> 00:07:58,296
If it prints true, Git found a filesystem
that ignores case when it created that

114
00:07:58,336 --> 00:08:03,493
repository, which is the macOS default. Then
the left column of this table describes

115
00:08:03,533 --> 00:08:04,359
your own disk.

116
00:08:04,699 --> 00:08:10,055
Read the root-cause line: actions/checkout
defaults to fetch-depth 1 and fetch-tags

117
00:08:10,095 --> 00:08:10,445
false.

118
00:08:12,285 --> 00:08:13,045
Into the lab.

119
00:08:13,385 --> 00:08:20,437
Case 1: a name that differs only in
case. Replay labs/run ch20b/case-clash.

120
00:08:20,777 --> 00:08:24,865
The developer's report: "The
loader test passes on my Mac.

121
00:08:24,905 --> 00:08:26,502
In CI: file not found."

122
00:08:26,842 --> 00:08:30,674
Look at the three results
together. core.ignorecase is true.

123
00:08:30,714 --> 00:08:34,287
Git recorded the name
Thresholds.yaml, with a capital T.

124
00:08:34,327 --> 00:08:39,127
And cat with a lower-case t opens the
file. On this machine the code works.

125
00:08:39,467 --> 00:08:41,650
Now the one command that proves the cause.

126
00:08:41,990 --> 00:08:46,676
git cat-file -e tests whether an object
exists at that path in the commit.

127
00:08:46,716 --> 00:08:47,251
It reads.

128
00:08:47,591 --> 00:08:53,173
Quick quiz. Git compares names byte by
byte. Which of the two commands succeeds?

129
00:08:53,213 --> 00:08:57,038
A, the lower-case name. B,
the name with the capital T.

130
00:08:57,078 --> 00:08:58,514
C, both. Your answer?

131
00:09:03,354 --> 00:09:08,729
B. 128 for the lower-case name, with a
message that is almost a diagnosis in itself:

132
00:09:08,769 --> 00:09:13,628
the path "exists on disk, but not in
HEAD". 0 for the name with the capital.

133
00:09:13,968 --> 00:09:17,138
Git answered the way a
case-sensitive filesystem will.

134
00:09:17,178 --> 00:09:22,131
If you said C, that's the Mac's answer, not
Git's. You've proven the cause without a

135
00:09:22,171 --> 00:09:22,547
runner.

136
00:09:22,887 --> 00:09:26,838
git mv is 🟢 SAFE: it renames
the file and its index entry.

137
00:09:26,878 --> 00:09:28,283
git commit is 🟢 SAFE.

138
00:09:28,623 --> 00:09:33,674
The status shows a rename, R, and after the
commit git ls-files shows the lower-case

139
00:09:33,714 --> 00:09:38,402
name. The comment in the transcript is
the point: the fix is a rename that Git

140
00:09:38,442 --> 00:09:42,099
records. Renaming in Finder
changes nothing that Git notices.

141
00:09:42,439 --> 00:09:47,148
The opposite accident: a commit made
on Linux that holds both README.md and

142
00:09:47,188 --> 00:09:52,339
Readme.md. A clone on this Mac prints a
warning that paths have collided, and only

143
00:09:52,379 --> 00:09:56,708
one of the two is in the working
tree. Two files in Git, one on disk.

144
00:09:57,048 --> 00:10:04,398
Case 2: a script with CRLF line endings.
Replay labs/run ch20b/line-endings.

145
00:10:04,738 --> 00:10:08,788
The developer's report: "The first
line of the deploy script fails."

146
00:10:09,128 --> 00:10:14,057
Exit status 127, and the message names an
interpreter: bash followed by a carriage

147
00:10:14,097 --> 00:10:19,026
return. The kernel read the first line of
the script and looked for a program with

148
00:10:19,066 --> 00:10:19,632
that name.

149
00:10:19,972 --> 00:10:21,876
The one command that proves the cause:

150
00:10:22,216 --> 00:10:27,695
The output has three columns per file. i/
is the index, which is what is committed.

151
00:10:27,735 --> 00:10:33,081
w/ is the working tree, the files on your
disk. attr/ is the attributes in force.

152
00:10:33,121 --> 00:10:36,473
What do you expect in the
i/ column for the script?

153
00:10:36,513 --> 00:10:37,536
Say it out loud.

154
00:10:42,376 --> 00:10:47,477
i/crlf. The committed blob itself
contains CRLF. The README is i/lf.

155
00:10:47,517 --> 00:10:53,373
The attribute column is empty for both,
and core.autocrlf is unset, so nothing

156
00:10:53,413 --> 00:10:58,287
normalised anything. Every clone
of this commit gets those bytes.

157
00:10:58,627 --> 00:11:04,018
git add --renormalize is 🟡 CAUTION: it
rewrites index entries to normalised line

158
00:11:04,058 --> 00:11:08,771
endings. The preview is git ls-files
--eol, and git status afterwards.

159
00:11:08,811 --> 00:11:13,184
Before committing you can take it
back with git restore --staged.

160
00:11:13,524 --> 00:11:17,160
After the commit, what will the
three columns say for the script?

161
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:18,348
Make your prediction.

162
00:11:23,188 --> 00:11:28,795
i/lf, the attribute text
eol=lf, and still w/crlf.

163
00:11:29,135 --> 00:11:33,969
The index is fixed. This working tree
still has the old bytes until the file is

164
00:11:34,009 --> 00:11:38,411
checked out again. A fresh clone,
which is what a runner makes, gets LF.

165
00:11:38,751 --> 00:11:41,955
The last snippet makes your
own working tree catch up.

166
00:11:41,995 --> 00:11:45,019
It deletes the file and
restores it from the index.

167
00:11:45,059 --> 00:11:48,503
git restore on a path is 🔴
DANGEROUS, so the five answers.

168
00:11:48,543 --> 00:11:52,228
What it changes: the file in the
working tree, from the index.

169
00:11:52,268 --> 00:11:56,793
What it can destroy: uncommitted content
of that file that was never staged.

170
00:11:57,133 --> 00:12:01,594
How to preview: git diff for the path.
How to recover: none for content that was

171
00:12:01,634 --> 00:12:06,151
never staged. When it's appropriate: here,
where the committed version is the one

172
00:12:06,191 --> 00:12:09,808
you want and git status shows
nothing else pending for that file.

173
00:12:10,148 --> 00:12:13,013
Now all three columns
agree, and the script runs.

174
00:12:13,353 --> 00:12:17,104
In the three-trees picture: the
working tree holds LF now, too.

175
00:12:17,444 --> 00:12:23,215
Case 3: a shallow clone has no
tags to describe. Replay labs/run

176
00:12:23,255 --> 00:12:25,303
ch20b/shallow-describe.

177
00:12:25,643 --> 00:12:30,578
The developer's report: "The
version step exits with 128 in CI."

178
00:12:30,918 --> 00:12:38,981
On the laptop: six commits, two tags,
and the description v1.1.0-2-g57c8425.

179
00:12:39,321 --> 00:12:43,948
Here's that history as a picture: six
commits in a row, two tags, and main on the

180
00:12:43,988 --> 00:12:47,462
newest. The runner is about to
receive only that last commit.

181
00:12:47,802 --> 00:12:49,227
git clone is 🟢 SAFE.

182
00:12:49,567 --> 00:12:53,535
One commit, marked "grafted".
No tags. Shallow: true.

183
00:12:53,575 --> 00:12:59,358
And git describe exits with 128: "No
names found". You have reproduced the CI

184
00:12:59,398 --> 00:13:02,004
failure in a directory on your Mac.

185
00:13:02,344 --> 00:13:07,312
Fetching the tags alone, still at depth
1: both tags exist now, and the error

186
00:13:07,352 --> 00:13:09,523
changes to "No tags can describe".

187
00:13:09,863 --> 00:13:13,723
The textbook's comment: both
messages mean the same root cause.

188
00:13:13,763 --> 00:13:15,642
The history is one commit long.

189
00:13:15,982 --> 00:13:20,046
🟢 SAFE: it adds objects and
tags and removes .git/shallow.

190
00:13:20,386 --> 00:13:24,296
Not shallow, six commits, and the
same description as on the laptop.

191
00:13:24,636 --> 00:13:29,769
In the workflow, the minimal fix is
fetch-depth: 0 on the checkout step of the one

192
00:13:29,809 --> 00:13:30,905
job that needs it.

193
00:13:32,745 --> 00:13:34,393
Five mistakes to watch for.

194
00:13:34,733 --> 00:13:38,069
Fixing a case problem by
renaming the file in Finder.

195
00:13:38,109 --> 00:13:43,356
Root cause: on a case-insensitive filesystem
Git does not see that as a change; the

196
00:13:43,396 --> 00:13:47,560
name in the index stays as it was
until git mv records the rename.

197
00:13:47,900 --> 00:13:53,201
Checking line endings by opening the file.
Root cause: the question is what the

198
00:13:53,241 --> 00:13:58,812
committed blob contains, and only the i/
column of git ls-files --eol answers that.

199
00:13:59,152 --> 00:14:04,538
Relying on each developer's core.autocrlf.
Root cause: that is local configuration;

200
00:14:04,578 --> 00:14:09,311
a rule in .gitattributes is in the
repository and applies to every clone.

201
00:14:09,651 --> 00:14:14,102
Adding --always or a fallback version
to make the describe step pass.

202
00:14:14,142 --> 00:14:19,440
Root cause: the step then ships a wrong
version; the cause is the shallow, tagless

203
00:14:19,480 --> 00:14:19,830
clone.

204
00:14:20,170 --> 00:14:24,527
Saying "it is the same commit" and stopping
there. Root cause: the filesystem, the

205
00:14:24,567 --> 00:14:28,816
depth of the clone, the commit the job
checked out and the shell are inputs too.

206
00:14:30,656 --> 00:14:35,582
Now, out of the lab. A team trains and
evaluates models from one repository, with

207
00:14:35,622 --> 00:14:40,670
engineers on Macs and CI on Linux. A new
engineer adds a configuration loader and a

208
00:14:40,710 --> 00:14:45,759
configuration file. The file was first saved
with a capital letter and added to Git

209
00:14:45,799 --> 00:14:46,310
that way.

210
00:14:46,650 --> 00:14:51,539
Later the engineer "renamed" it in the
editor's file tree, and the code refers to

211
00:14:51,579 --> 00:14:56,588
the lower-case name. Every test passes on
every Mac in the team for a week, because

212
00:14:56,628 --> 00:15:01,638
each Mac answers for both spellings. The
first pull request that makes CI load that

213
00:15:01,678 --> 00:15:03,524
file fails with file-not-found.

214
00:15:03,864 --> 00:15:07,640
Step 2 of the investigation
order says the commit is right.

215
00:15:07,680 --> 00:15:13,008
Step 4 says the machine differs. One command,
git ls-files for the directory, shows

216
00:15:13,048 --> 00:15:17,923
the recorded name. One git mv, one
commit, and it's fixed for every machine.

217
00:15:18,263 --> 00:15:23,003
The prevention the team adopts is a check
that asks Git, not the disk, for the names

218
00:15:23,043 --> 00:15:23,857
the code needs.

219
00:15:25,697 --> 00:15:30,743
Your turn. Do Lab 28.1,
"Six broken workflows", in

220
00:15:30,783 --> 00:15:34,710
lab-manual/m28-runners-debugging-ci.md.

221
00:15:35,050 --> 00:15:39,740
The lab tells you how to start: first
write, for each of the six files, your

222
00:15:39,780 --> 00:15:44,781
hypothesis and the line you suspect. Only
then collect the evidence Git can give.

223
00:15:44,821 --> 00:15:50,010
For each, also write the layer the cause
belongs to: Git, GitHub, or GitHub Actions.

224
00:15:50,350 --> 00:15:54,415
The challenge is Incident 7,
incidents/07-ci-passes-locally.

225
00:15:54,455 --> 00:16:00,093
Read the symptoms file only. The generator
script is the answer to "what happened".

226
00:16:03,933 --> 00:16:08,849
Answer out loud. A strong answer names the
suspected mechanism in one sentence and

227
00:16:08,889 --> 00:16:13,925
then gives the proof as commands that read
what Git has recorded, not what is on the

228
00:16:13,965 --> 00:16:18,881
disk, and says what result confirms it. It
gives the fix as something Git records,

229
00:16:18,921 --> 00:16:23,957
and says why a rename outside Git doesn't
work. The follow-up reverses the accident,

230
00:16:23,997 --> 00:16:28,973
with a commit from Linux that holds two
names differing only in case, and then asks

231
00:16:29,013 --> 00:16:32,055
which other Git-level cause
the chapter reproduces.

232
00:16:32,095 --> 00:16:33,928
You've seen both in this video.

233
00:16:35,768 --> 00:16:36,695
Let's land this.

234
00:16:37,035 --> 00:16:38,695
You should now be able to say:

235
00:16:39,035 --> 00:16:43,856
The same commit is not the same input: the
filesystem, the depth of the clone, the

236
00:16:43,896 --> 00:16:46,939
commit the job checked out
and the shell all differ.

237
00:16:47,279 --> 00:16:52,665
A case problem is proven with git cat-file
-e or git ls-files, which compare names

238
00:16:52,705 --> 00:16:55,047
byte by byte, and fixed with git mv.

239
00:16:55,387 --> 00:17:01,271
A line-ending problem is proven with git
ls-files --eol, where i/crlf means the

240
00:17:01,311 --> 00:17:07,345
committed blob has CRLF, and fixed with
.gitattributes and git add --renormalize.

241
00:17:07,685 --> 00:17:12,665
A shallow-clone problem is reproduced
with a clone at depth 1 without tags, and

242
00:17:12,705 --> 00:17:16,224
fixed with fetch-depth: 0 in
the job that needs history.

243
00:17:16,564 --> 00:17:20,132
Each cause has a Git-side test that
needs no re-run of the workflow.

244
00:17:21,972 --> 00:17:30,354
Read section 20B.12 of Chapter 20B. Do
Exercise 28.6, "The machine changed, the code

245
00:17:30,394 --> 00:17:35,467
did not", in
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

246
00:17:35,807 --> 00:17:40,712
Three reports, three proofs, and not one
re-run. The next time someone says "it's

247
00:17:40,752 --> 00:17:45,292
the same commit", you have four questions
ready. Try the --eol listing on a

248
00:17:45,332 --> 00:17:50,359
repository of your own. Next time: required
checks that stay pending, the debugging

249
00:17:50,399 --> 00:17:54,267
instruments, linting, and what a
